# A tool-gated verb refused bare-handed: the refusal names the tool, and the action stays the way on.
#
# Reported from a real run: cutting the heart out of a corpse with no knife was refused, the persona
# re-voiced "I cannot cut a heart: I would need a knife for that" as "I hold still, waiting. It
# breaks." — the knife gone — and the action was then drawn greyed out yet still clickable, so a
# second Execute was the same refusal charged again. Three things are asserted here:
#
#   * the refusal sentence is the longer, explicit one (NeutralNarration.ToolRequired): what was
#     wanted, that bare hands cannot do it, which tool it takes, and that it was left undone —
#     never "find" or "take it in hand", which came back as done. Under --playground the rewrite
#     is a stand-in, so this asserts the neutral text the persona is handed — the half a test can see;
#   * clicking the action again offers "Use Tool" only — Execute is disabled;
#   * "Use Tool" with the knife then carries the cut through.
#
# The corpse is made the way cli/verb/cut/success.cli makes it: slay the chicken, knife combined.
#
# FLAGS: --playground --skip-childhood --debug --seed 42 --no-encounters --auto-dialogue --grant-item knife --location-type test --location-id 0 --npc-static --start-area "Test Yard" --period noon --observe-only "chicken"

wait
click menu New
wait mode ProtagonistCreation 20
click continue
wait mode WorldView 60
travel here
wait mode LocationInteraction 60

# ── Make the corpse ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
strategy succeed
goal slay
advance 12 180
click keyword 0
choose 0
choose ripelore
advance 12 180
click action 0
choose 1
choose 0
advance 12 180
click action 1
choose 0
advance 12 180
click continue
advance 12 180
expect Slain:

# ── Cut it bare-handed ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
click button
advance 12 180
goal cut
click keyword 0
choose 0
choose ripelore
advance 12 180

# The phase carried on past the first act (NarrativeController.ContinueNarrationSegment): its
# buttons are still on screen, spent, and keep their numbers, so the next act's button follows them.
click action 2
choose 0
advance 12 180
# Short fragments, because `expect` matches one row and the sentence wraps.
expect my bare hands could not do it
expect it takes a knife
expect So I left it undone
expect-not I would need to find

# ── The action is still the way on, and only through a tool ──────────────────
click action 2
inspect popup
expect-state popup Execute(disabled)
expect-no-state popup Use Tool(disabled)
choose 1
choose 0
advance 12 180
click action 3
choose 0
advance 12 180
click continue
advance 12 180
expect Item received:
expect-verb cut
quit
